[size=4][b]Flashlight[/b][/size]
A flashlight for Stardew Valley that casts a beam in the direction you face and runs on batteries. Buy it early, keep it in your backpack, and see at night without holding a torch.

[size=3][b]What it adds[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Flashlight[/b] (a tool): sold at Robin's Carpenter's Shop, one to a player, 600g. Press F (or use the tool) to switch it on and off. It keeps shining while you use other tools, which a torch cannot do. A charge bar under its picture works like the water bar of the watering can.
[*][b]Battery slots[/b]: two, always two, like the bait slot on a fishing rod. It runs on a matching pair and needs both loaded, like a real flashlight: two Dry Cells or two Battery Packs, never one of each. Right-click the flashlight with cells on the cursor to load them and right-click it with an empty hand to take one out.
[*][b]Dry Cell[/b] (a battery): sold at Robin's in limited numbers, or crafted from 1 Copper Bar and 1 Coal for 12 cells once you reach Mining level 2. A pair lasts 2 in-game hours, and only while it is dark where you are.
[*]A cone of light 7 tiles long in the direction you face, like a real flashlight, with nothing lit around you. It flickers now and then like an old flashlight with a loose cell, and much more often when the charge is nearly gone.
[*][b]Held in your hand[/b]: you are drawn holding it while it is selected. It follows your hand as you walk and the beam starts at its lens.
[*][b]Battery Pack[/b] (the game's own): a pair of them goes in the slots instead. A pair lasts 4 hours, two more than Dry Cells, and makes the beam 20 percent longer and a little brighter. Found from Lightning Rods and Solar Panels, never sold.
[*]No beam during cutscenes and festivals. It switches off when you go to bed.
[/list]

[size=3][b]Balance[/b][/size]
It reaches further than a torch, but only forward, and it costs upkeep, so it does not replace the Glow Rings. One flashlight to a player, batteries are limited each day and sell for little, so they cannot be hoarded or flipped for profit. The reasoning is written down in the docs on GitHub.

[size=3][b]Settings[/b][/size]
Everything below can be changed in game with Generic Mod Config Menu (optional), or in config.json: toggle key (keyboard or controller buttons), beam length, battery life, battery slots, prices, daily stock, chance of a thin shelf, and flicker.

[size=3][b]Feedback[/b][/size]
The numbers are a first pass. Tell me how it plays: how many nights a pair of Dry Cells lasted, whether the shop felt too tight or too generous. Type [b]flashlight_report[/b] in the SMAPI console and paste what it prints. It shows your changed settings and a local tally of use, and nothing is sent anywhere. Bugs and balance notes also go on GitHub.

[size=3][b]Install[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Install SMAPI 4.2.1 or newer.
[*]Unzip the Flashlight folder into Stardew Valley/Mods.
[/list]
Needs Stardew Valley 1.6.9 or newer. Works with the game's own content, no Content Patcher needed.

[size=3][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]It uses a few small Harmony patches on the game's own code (battery slots, charge bar, tooltip, the one-flashlight rule). They only act on the flashlight, so SMAPI lists the mod as one that changes game code.
[*]If another mod controls Robin's shop, turn off "Sold at Robin's" in the settings.
[*]Multiplayer is untested. Other players will see the ordinary round light instead of the cone.
[*]Tested on Windows. Linux and Mac are untested.
[/list]

[size=3][b]Uninstalling[/b][/size]
Remove the folder. Any flashlights and Dry Cells in a save turn into error items until you load the save with the mod again, so sell or drop them first.

